A||||7||86se/lambo||Catco Cat high flow what do you think||||1||
Z||000000||86se/lambo||05-22-2001||08:47 PM||||I booght a catco high flow converter from summit racing ($81) and thought wow this thing is really wimping lookink... and kinda restrictive. I wonder if the other units(i.e. chris west, etc..) are any better? do we have any dyno numbers on different hi-flow cats?<BR>i plan on using the hi-flow cat with a 12 inch glass-pak and monza-twin tips w/resonators. I have read some of the past threads and could not quite find the answer i wanted.... can ya help?<BR>i'm going to call cat-co to send me some figures regarding HOW much better is the flow over the stock...hollowed out converter.<BR>thanks<BR>mildly modified 2.8||||reg||1
Z||000001||cadaver||05-22-2001||09:58 PM||||Your asking about restrictions and you are going to use glass packs. Those ARE restrictive.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MikeSchaefer||05-23-2001||12:30 AM||||I imagine that setup would be a LOT less restrictive than stock though.  <BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||05-23-2001||08:19 AM||||Catco makes GREAT stuff.. thats all the Shop uses on their street/strip cars. I've got one on my car 2.5" and i've driven cars with the 3" units installed... and not one of them have failed... and they are more resiliant(sp) to nitrous. where the Random units will be empty after a month of use... (thats what we've found) Glas packs (bullet mufflers)  are ok but they cause resonance and aren't the best for performance... <A HREF="http://www.spintechmufflers.com" TARGET=_blank>www.spintechmufflers.com</A>  <A HREF="http://www.brullenexhaust.com" TARGET=_blank>www.brullenexhaust.com</A>  <A HREF="http://www.powertone.com" TARGET=_blank>www.powertone.com</A>   <P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig1fst.jpg">        <A HREF="http://www.engineered.net" TARGET=_blank>www.engineered.net</A>  <A HREF="http://www.epdyno.com" TARGET=_blank>www.epdyno.com</A>  <A HREF="http://www.enthuzacar.com" TARGET=_blank>www.enthuzacar.com</A> <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 05-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||86se/lambo||05-26-2001||01:06 AM||||i spoke with mike at catco nad he informed me that his cat flows "much" better than sock...but he did not have flow data on our specific unit. he did say that this unit can handle flow up to 5.9 liters and that it is the "brick" style which is much better than the pellets from g.m. he also said a hollowed out cat will flow better..but may not have some of the emission qualities.<BR>i am going to bypass the flowmaster(well atleast for now) to try and keep engine bay temps down and hopefully gain a little extra power. i choose the monza 3" twin tips w/resonators for that throaty ferrari/lamborghini sound.<BR>thanks for the input.||||reg||10
Z||000005||87GTZ34||05-26-2001||03:01 PM||||While on the topic... I went to 2 exhaust shops today to chat w/ them and see if I could glean anything from them w/ respect to their competency.  One shop told me that all after market cats are hi-flo???  The other shop showed me their Walker/Dynomax (Teneco) unit.  You could hold it up to your eye and see light thru it.  Any opinions???  I was looking for the same info on flow capabilities but not too many will tell you.  Its more like 'trust me on this, we have the highest flowing unit'.||||reg||5
Z||000006||87GTZ34||05-26-2001||09:50 PM||||Bumpity-bump...  Maybe it was buried.<P>My question is, does anyone have experience or opinions of the walker/dynomax catalytic converter?  Or am I the only one that runs a cat these days?||||reg||5
Z||000007||terryk||05-27-2001||12:11 AM||||I'm using both Catco's and Walker and I see no difference. I haven't found any real difference in flows between any cats for a given pipe size. I'm sure there is some crap out there somewhere!<BR>||||reg||1
